; S1a.3.1 — an `(absent …)` whose sub-plan compiles to nothing.
;
; `?x` is not a premise: the premise compiler returns no steps for it, and an
; empty sub-plan does not mean "a query that finds nothing" — it matches
; VACUOUSLY, so the matcher's `holds` is true and the guard fails against every
; possible KB. The guard is monotone (no nested absent), so the candidate is
; retired permanently on its first judgement: a guard that can never pass.
;
; Compile-negative until M1e2 S1e2.2.24 (W24, Q-M1e2.18 (v)): the compiler
; refused the empty sub-plan with a CompileError in the Python IR's spelling,
; `(absent …) sub-plan compiled to no steps in SForm(…)`. Since then the
; loader refuses `?x` as a premise that is not a form, before the compiler,
; and this file moved here from `examples/broken/compile/`.
(relation p Thing)

(rule vacuous-guard ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent ?x))
  :assert (p ?x))
